-- title:
--   Theorem 1, p.5 — Algorithm 1 is well-defined; iterates stay in $\mathcal S$
-- statement:
--   Assume the standing assumptions of §2: $\gamma_0>0$, $\delta\in(0,1)$, $0<\nu\le1/2$, $\eta\in[0,\nu/(1+\nu))$, $\epsilon>0$, $x^0\in\mathrm{dom}\,B$, Assumption 1 with $\mu>0$, and let $x^*$ be the solution of (1). Then:
--
--   1. Algorithm 1 is well-defined at each iteration: after any number of iterations without termination, the line search of the next iteration has a smallest accepted integer.
--   2. $x^t\in\mathcal S$ for every generated $x^t$, and
--   $$
--   \sum_{i=1}^tn_i\le M+t\qquad\text{for every completed iteration }t,\qquad M=\Bigl\lceil\frac{\log(\xi/(\gamma_0L_{\widehat{\mathcal S}}))}{\log\delta}\Bigr\rceil_+.
--   $$
--
--   This is the core of the analysis of Algorithm 1: it closes the induction between Lemma 5 and Lemma 7.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: $F$ is a point-valued operator on $\mathrm{cl}(\mathrm{dom}\,B)$ → a total map on $\mathbb R^n$, constrained only on $\mathrm{cl}(\mathrm{dom}\,B)$; reason: values of $F$ elsewhere never enter $F+B$ or a run, since iterates lie in $\mathrm{dom}\,B$
--   - encoding: Assumption 1(d), the resolvent of $\gamma B$ can be exactly evaluated → a family $J$ with $J_\gamma=(I+\gamma B)^{-1}$ for every $\gamma>0$; reason: the assumption has no mathematical content; the family exists and is unique on $\gamma>0$
--   - encoding: Algorithm 1 is well-defined at each iteration → every non-terminated prefix has a smallest accepted integer for its next iteration; reason: the existence of $n_t$ is the only step of an iteration that can fail; the rest is explicit, given $J$
--   - generalization: $\sum_{i=1}^tn_i\le M+t$ for $1\le t\in\mathbb T-1$ → also at $t=0$; reason: there it reads $0\le M$; the printed range is a special case
--   - encoding: $\lceil\omega\rceil_+$ → `Nat.ceil`; reason: $(\lceil\omega\rceil)_+$ is the natural-number ceiling, for every real $\omega$
--   - generalization: $L_{\mathcal S},L_{\widehat{\mathcal S}}$ are the constants given by Lemma 1, for some choice → every pair valid in the sense of Lemma 1; reason: the proof uses only the two Lipschitz bounds, and Lemma 1 supplies such a pair, so the printed statement is the instance at that pair
--   - encoding: $\{x^t\}_{t\in\mathbb T}$ and $\{n_t\}_{1\le t\in\mathbb T-1}$ generated by Algorithm 1 → the clauses quantify over every prefix run `Alg1.IsPrefix` at index $t$, which fixes $x^0,\dots,x^{t+1}$ and $n_1,\dots,n_t$; reason: the run is relational and deterministic ($n_t$ least, $J$ a function), so the prefixes are exactly the initial segments of the generated sequence
-- source:
--   Lu & Mei, Primal-dual extrapolation methods for monotone inclusions under local Lipschitz continuity, arXiv:2206.00973v3, Theorem 1, p.5

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ThreeOpSplitting_Convergence_MonotoneOperators
import Definitions.Def_MonoIncExtrap_Setting
import Definitions.Def_MonoIncExtrap_Algorithm1

open InnerProductSpace ThreeOpSplitting.Convergence

namespace MonoIncExtrap

/-- **Theorem 1.** arXiv:2206.00973v3, Theorem 1, p.5. (i) Algorithm 1 is well-defined at each
iteration: after `t ≥ 0` iterations without termination, the line search of the next iteration has
a smallest accepted integer. (ii) All iterates lie in `S`, and `Σ_{i=1}^t n_i ≤ M + t`.

**Formalization Note.** Index `t = 0` is included: (i) there is iteration 1, and (ii) reads
`x⁰, x¹ ∈ S` and `0 ≤ M`. `M` is built from any valid pair of Lemma 1. -/
theorem theorem_1 {n : ℕ}
    {B : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) → Set (EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n))}
    {I : Alg1.Input (EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n))} {xstar : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)}
    (hI : Alg1.Standing B I xstar) :
    (∀ (x : ℕ → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)) (γ : ℕ → ℝ) (n' : ℕ → ℕ) (t : ℕ),
      Alg1.IsPrefix I x γ n' t → (1 ≤ t → ¬ Alg1.StoppedAt I x γ t) →
        ∃ m : ℕ, IsLeast {m | I.Accepts (x t) (x (t + 1)) (γ t) m} m) ∧
    (∀ (x : ℕ → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)) (γ : ℕ → ℝ) (n' : ℕ → ℕ) (t : ℕ),
      Alg1.IsPrefix I x γ n' t →
        (∀ i ≤ t + 1, x i ∈ Alg1.setS B xstar (I.r0 xstar) I.ν) ∧
        ∀ LS LShat : ℝ, Alg1.ValidS B I xstar LS LShat →
          ∑ i ∈ Finset.Icc 1 t, n' i ≤ I.Mconst LShat + t) := by sorry

end MonoIncExtrap
